36da7154f6b86ec.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" :question/worked-solution "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/9fb8c20672705e9d68e69d32e7ff3499ec71560e10aa85136b3f3ad75c5fb5ed.png)\n\nNotice that to transform $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we shift $y=u(t)$ by $3$ units to the left, giving $y=u(t+3).$\n\n- There is no vertical scaling or offset.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=u(t+3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152479 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f7a33f8-b0f6-5265-9d3e-c1ca34c07161" :knowledge-point/title "Graphs of Transformed Step Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1cb84a3-549d-594a-82ff-2a06cecb2df0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149715} {:answer/id #uuid "ca5146be-d10f-5232-8c82-42df43979d39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2u(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149716} {:answer/id #uuid "197e0a11-e23f-518f-9db4-c9f4a7cbd614" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2u(t - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149717} {:answer/id #uuid "2ffc47f5-1192-5d9c-ae20-15f8d828db63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2u(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149718} {:answer/id #uuid "666b50d8-b1c9-57d0-a1be-a32919cd4de2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2u(t) + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ffc47f5-1192-5d9c-ae20-15f8d828db63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2u(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149718} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc7ca6ff-c9f5-5e7a-9cae-ff55643497ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93d8abe5-b394-5689-9e86-76b2052077e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-331249" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/bcf4076849dd96858c586f8696414789b3f68074b87652601245407142f2b8fc.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" :question/worked-solution "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/9fb8c20672705e9d68e69d32e7ff3499ec71560e10aa85136b3f3ad75c5fb5ed.png)\n\nNotice that to transform $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we scale $y=u(t)$ by a factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y=2u(t).$\n\n- There is no horizontal shift or offset.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=2u(t).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "253b1011-a313-5004-8cbe-61133586e6b1" :knowledge-point/title "Graphs of Reflected Step Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b59a5f2-08d2-53a2-8d02-197f9b6491d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305205} {:answer/id #uuid "feed8e4e-ac40-59db-b227-a446140c2cce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2u(1-t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305206} {:answer/id #uuid "9a3c0ea6-2003-5f75-8604-a7a0b20f38f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(1-t)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305207} {:answer/id #uuid "b67bd27d-0472-5836-b595-e122a08c69a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(-1-t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305208} {:answer/id #uuid "6aadd213-8803-5d03-99a0-d02baaa4acba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(1-t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6aadd213-8803-5d03-99a0-d02baaa4acba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(1-t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305209} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9db1c33-43d0-5cbf-9a18-59bf7f9b0dc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ceb1494-1a1f-5de9-ba32-14cee7c1a38d" :question/math-academy-id "q-331267" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/c3680f88195487cb34a0c288dc605bbd731868433f52317a7cd31797caad3dd2.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" :question/worked-solution "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/a1748c6e23d99ad6ef43d5aceedcadb7a12fcdbef5064f93202d2fe5ca3d98d7.png)\n\nNotice that to transform $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we reflect $y=u(t)$ in the $y$-axis, which gives $y=u(-t).$\n\n- Next, we shift by one unit to the right, giving $y=u(-(t-1))=u(1-t).$\n\n- There is no vertical scaling or offset.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=u(1-t).$"}] [{:db